  "query_preview": "..\nSunday through Tuesday: There is good model agreement that a\nvertically stacked low will move to just off the Washington\ncoast on Sunday before weakening and moving inland along the\nWA/OR border on Monday. As it moves in increased mid level\nmoisture and lift will result in a band of showers moving across\nthe Inland NW with snow levels around 5000 to 6000 feet. With\nthe weakening nature of the low and quick movement through the\narea precipitation amounts are expected to be light, mainly a\ntenth of an inch or less. Exception is around the Blue Mountains\ninto the Central Panhandle Mountains where stronger lift as well\nas surface based instability with CAPE of 200-400 J/KG has the \npotential to produce locally heavier downpours with rain amounts\nof a quarter inch. A few thunderstorms can not be ruled out but\nconfidence remains too low to include in the forecast.\n\nWednesday through Saturday: An upper ridge amplifies along 150W\nin the Eastern Pacific forcing a trough to settle into the\nregion, with a stronger closed low dropping south along the WA\ncoast on Wednesday and remaining in place Thursday. There is \ngood ensemble agreement leading to high confidence of a cooler \nand showery pattern as snow levels fall by Thursday to \n3000-4500 feet. This will allow for snow in the mountains \nincluding the passes, especially in the Cascades and Okanogan \nHighlands given closer proximity to the low. By 5 PM Thursday, \nthe NBM chances for 2 inches or more of snow is 90 percent for \nSherman Pass, 80% chance Stevens Pass, 70% Washington Pass, and \n35% Lookout Pass. The forecast gets more uncertainty after that \nwith some models keeping the low over the region while others \nbegins to show it moving out. Regardless temperatures will be \ntrending cooler for this part of the forecast with high \ntemperatures dropping into the 40s to low 50s, and lows dropping\ninto the mid 20s to mid 30s. With overnight lows near freezing\nin the valleys some snow may mix in at times but accumulations\nare expected to remain in the mountains. JW",
